KPL/MYB

IAU 2006/2000A celestial intermediate pole (CIP) and origin (CIO): the series of the CIP
coordinates X, Y in the GCRS and of s + XY/2, with the fundamental arguments, the Earth
rotation angle and the TIO locator s'. Read by myb_cip_create (myb/frames.h) with the
prefix 'IAU2006A'; the library itself knows none of these numbers.

Sources
  IERS Conventions (2010), IERS Technical Note 36, ch. 5 (G. Petit, B. Luzum, eds.):
    eq. 5.15 (ERA, IAU 2000 Resolution B1.8), 5.13 (s'), 5.16 (series form),
    5.43 (Delaunay arguments, Simon et al. 1994), 5.44 (planetary arguments,
    Souchay et al. 1999; p_A of Kinoshita & Souchay 1990).
  Tables of the IERS Conventions Centre, as downloaded on 2026-11-09 from
    https://iers-conventions.obspm.fr/content/chapter5/additional_info/:
      tab5.2a.txt  SHA-256 19549252df9eb77c8237dbf5b749de82b7fd7713a8b60b35371538cd36b6aa1d
      tab5.2b.txt  SHA-256 1f17a3a6ad0b468705b3323bfe72d0320ce996798ec7cba0cdde4405d88f14d9
      tab5.2d.txt  SHA-256 fe94c83e1ef6f92b15b3f007779ae70c0984c06ee45d5511b4c821ee9a0ecded
    (IAU 2006 precession and IAU 2000A_R06 nutation; the polynomial parts are those of
    the 2006 adjustment, identical to SOFA/ERFA xy06 and s06).
  Numbers are copied without change; amplitudes and polynomials in microarcseconds.

Form (myb_cip_create)
  t = (TT - J2000.0) in Julian centuries of 36525 days.
  Argument k (k = 1 .. COUNT): F_k(t) = sum_i POLYNOMIAL[5 (k-1) + i] t^i, i = 0 .. 4, in the
  unit of ARGUMENT_SCALE[k] radians, reduced modulo ARGUMENT_TURN[k] (one revolution in
  that unit) before conversion: 1296000 arcseconds for l, l', F, D, Omega; 2 pi radians
  for the planetary longitudes L_Me .. L_Ne and the precession p_A.
  Series Q = X, Y, S (S = s + XY/2):
    Q(t) = Q_SCALE * ( sum_i Q_POLYNOMIAL[i] t^i
                      + sum_terms t^j (a_s sin ARG + a_c cos ARG) ),  ARG = sum_k n_k F_k,
  each term being 3 + COUNT numbers: j, a_s, a_c, n_1 .. n_COUNT.
  ERA = 2 pi (ERA[0] + Tu + ERA[1] Tu), Tu = (UT1 - J2000.0) in days (eq. 5.15).
  s' = SPRIME_RATE * t * X_SCALE (eq. 5.13, -47 microarcseconds per century).

\begindata

